Here is the website which I need to partially automate as exercise. Tools: VS Code, Playwright with Typescript.\
https://the-internet.herokuapp.com/\
Rules:\
- no comments in the code\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 - Error messages should be present for every assert. Not long, but not too laconic either.\
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf2 - there should be PageObjects and tests. Their paths for the imports: \
../../pages/theinternet/\
../../tests/theinternet/\
- Have a clear name that describes behavior, not steps ("locked user sees error", not "test login")\
- Be independent from other tests \'97 no shared state, no execution order dependency\
Include at least one meaningful assertion (using web-first assertions: toBeVisible, toHaveText, toHaveURL, toHaveCount)\
- Avoid waitForTimeout \'97 always\
Look for stable attributes:\
data-testid \'97 added by developers for testing\
aria-label \'97 accessible label for screen readers\
role + accessible name \'97 for example, a button with text "Login"\
id \'97 good only if it is stable and unique\
- no test.only\
- Never use xpath\
- CSS selectors as locators are forbidden too\
Organize tests with test.describe, beforeEach, and hooks\
It is fine to expose locators like errorMessage or cartBadge as readonly properties \'97 tests need them for assertions. But user actions (click, fill, navigate) should be wrapped in methods like login(), addProductToCart().\
Do not over-engineer. Do not create BasePage, custom wrappers, or complex abstractions.\'a0\
Write tests that are readable, maintainable, and well-structured\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
